        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 679,
            "height": 706,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "ceil (ceiling) rounds each value UP to the whole number at or above it - towards positive.\n\n2.2 comes out as 3, 2.0 as 2, -2.5 as -2 (up, which for a negative number is towards zero). In the demo a sine wave from -3 to 3 becomes a staircase that always sits at or above the wave.\n\nUse it when a part needs a whole one: how many pages for 41 lines at 10 a page (4.1, so 5), how many buses for a group, how many tiles to cover a length.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their shape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through ceil cell by cell (right).\n\nnp.ceil is the same node under its NumPy name, and behaves identically.\n\nSYNTAX:\nceil\n\nEXAMPLES:\n/ 10 then ceil     pages needed for a number of lines, 10 to a page\nceil       the next whole number up\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input rounded up. A single number comes out as a whole number.\n\nTHE FOUR ROUNDING NODES:\nThey differ in which way a fraction goes. Given -2.5 and 2.7:\nround   -2   3     to the nearest whole number\nfloor   -3   2     down, towards negative\nceil    -2   3     up, towards positive\ntrunc   -2   2     towards zero - the fraction is simply dropped\nFor positive numbers trunc is the same as floor; for negative numbers it is the same as ceil.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nfloor   round down\ntrunc   round towards zero\nround   round to the nearest",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 671,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "ceil (ceiling) rounds each value UP to the whole number at or above it - towards positive.\n\n2.2 comes out as 3, 2.0 as 2, -2.5 as -2 (up, which for a negative number is towards zero). In the demo a sine wave from -3 to 3 becomes a staircase that always sits at or above the wave.\n\nUse it when a part needs a whole one: how many pages for 41 lines at 10 a page (4.1, so 5), how many buses for a group, how many tiles to cover a length.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their shape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through ceil cell by cell (right).\n\nnp.ceil is the same node under its NumPy name, and behaves identically.\n\nSYNTAX:\nceil\n\nEXAMPLES:\n/ 10 then ceil     pages needed for a number of lines, 10 to a page\nceil       the next whole number up\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input rounded up. A single number comes out as a whole number.\n\nTHE FOUR ROUNDING NODES:\nThey differ in which way a fraction goes. Given -2.5 and 2.7:\nround   -2   3     to the nearest whole number\nfloor   -3   2     down, towards negative\nceil    -2   3     up, towards positive\ntrunc   -2   2     towards zero - the fraction is simply dropped\nFor positive numbers trunc is the same as floor; for negative numbers it is the same as ceil.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nfloor   round down\ntrunc   round towards zero\nround   round to the nearest"
        },
